1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,000
What happens when the next module needs the same security and the same Keycloak calls as the first?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,940
Right now the security rules live in the app, and the Keycloak calls live inside the partner module.

3
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:16,464
Copy them into a second module, and every fix must be made twice.

4
00:00:16,464 --> 00:00:22,166
So both become platform libraries, published like the error and OTP libraries before them.

5
00:00:22,166 --> 00:00:29,940
The security library secures any app: access tokens, realm roles, and refusals as problem details.

6
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:35,142
The route rules move into configuration, and anything not listed is still refused.

7
00:00:35,142 --> 00:00:40,571
It configures itself before Spring Boot's own defaults, and an app can still replace it.

8
00:00:40,571 --> 00:00:47,095
The Keycloak library is a small admin client that logs in as its own client and refreshes its token.

9
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:52,000
The partner module keeps its own identity port, and now simply delegates.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,797
Each library has its own tests: route rules with fake tokens, and the admin client against a real Keycloak.

11
00:00:58,797 --> 00:01:04,500
Nothing changed for callers: the full onboarding test and the load tests pass unchanged.

12
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:08,797
Next, the money rules begin, with the commission on every payment.

